      "math_academy_id": "q-184064",
      "sequence_position": 17,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=-8p+2q+7,$ where $p,q\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.\n\nThe number $n$ is {{field-1}} since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =-8p+2q+7 \\\\  & =2(-4p+{{field-2}})+1 \\\\  & ={{field-3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=${{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $-8p+2q+7:$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $7=6+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{odd}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =-8p+2q+7 \\\\  & =(-8p+2q+6)+1 \\\\  & =2({{-4p+q+3}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=-4p+q+3\\in Z.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
